1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,620
Loopback Transformer:

2
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:05,970
towards a unified view of
residual looped Transformers.

3
00:00:07,470 --> 00:00:12,480
Looped Transformers save parameters by
running the same blocks again and again.

4
00:00:13,030 --> 00:00:17,180
But sharing alone doesn't decide how
all that repeated computation is reused.

5
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:21,570
Think of residual
connections as depth memory.

6
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:26,190
Addition lets the total update
weight keep growing with depth,

7
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:29,970
and a joint softmax makes the
input compete with the updates.

8
00:00:31,610 --> 00:00:33,340
Loopback decouples the two:

9
00:00:33,770 --> 00:00:35,700
it stores each block's update,

10
00:00:36,290 --> 00:00:38,360
keeps the input on its own path,

11
00:00:38,850 --> 00:00:42,440
and lets decay set the total weight
while attention allocates it.

12
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:45,570
The result:

13
00:00:45,940 --> 00:00:49,290
from 191M to 3B parameters,

14
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:51,989
Loopback reaches lower loss and a higher

15
00:00:51,989 --> 00:00:55,010
thirteen-task average than
the standard Transformer,

16
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:58,490
with only 40–75% of its parameters.

17
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:05,450
Against the standard Transformer and
seven other looped residual designs,

18
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:08,396
Loopback has the lowest
loss and the highest

19
00:01:08,396 --> 00:01:10,910
thirteen-task average at all three scales.

20
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:14,580
At 1.4B and 3B,

21
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:18,000
it also predicts math and
code solutions better,

22
00:01:18,370 --> 00:01:19,660
and at 3B,

23
00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:25,140
word-problem accuracy
rises from 2.6% to 6.4%.

24
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:29,750
Run more loops at
inference than in training,

25
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:34,950
and Loopback degrades far less than
Plain Loop at all three scales.

26
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:38,050
Both controls matter:

27
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:39,080
without attention,

28
00:01:39,150 --> 00:01:40,080
the loss rises;

29
00:01:40,510 --> 00:01:41,360
without decay,

30
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:42,720
it rises much more.

31
00:01:43,430 --> 00:01:45,780
And the gains hold across initializations.

32
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:49,240
At a fixed parameter budget,

33
00:01:49,510 --> 00:01:52,580
deeper and narrower standard
Transformers lose ground,

34
00:01:52,870 --> 00:01:55,100
while Loopback's margin grows with depth.

35
00:01:57,180 --> 00:01:58,550
The cost is small:

36
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:03,070
reading the stored updates
adds under 0.1% in FLOPs,

37
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:05,590
and at 3B,

38
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:09,370
training runs within 6% of the
standard Transformer's speed.

39
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:12,630
Loopback stores updates,

40
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:14,990
keeps the input on its own path,

41
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:17,990
and lets decay and attention
control their weight:

42
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:20,210
lower loss with fewer parameters.

